            <ArticleTitle>Risk factors for scholastic backwardness in children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>655-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To identify the risk factors for scholastic backwardness in children.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Children in the 6-12 year age group attending regular schools and referred to the child guidance clinic for scholastic backwardness.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Tertiary care hospital in South India.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Participants were compared with an age-and sex- matched group of children with good academic performance, to ascertain risk factors for scholastic backwardness.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There were 75 boys and 35 girls in the study group. Among them 30 (27%) children had mental retardation, 39 (36%) had borderline intelligence and 12 (11%) had microcephaly. Undernutrition was noted in 36 (33%) children and 31 (28%) had stunted growth. 28 (26%) children had a history of chronic medical problems like epilepsy, bronchial asthma and congenital heart diseases. Visual, hearing and speech defects were present in 6 (6%), 5 (5%) and 12 (11%) children, respectively. Statistically significant differences were noted in the educational level and employment status of parents of children with scholastic backwardness and those with good academic performance.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Social and family factors have a significant influence on the academic functioning of children.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
